<title>SOUTH AFRICA: Hicor half year shr dips as taxman bites.</title>
<headline>Hicor half year shr dips as taxman bites.</headline>
<dateline>JOHANNESBURG 1996-10-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>Half year ended June 30</p>
<p>			(million rand unless stated)</p>
<p>    Shr, attrib (cts)     1.9	   vs 2.3</p>
<p>    Net operating income  4.11	  vs 2.95</p>
<p>    Finance charges	 1.25	  vs 0.52</p>
<p>    Pre-tax		   2.86	  vs 2.43</p>
<p>    Tax			 0.93	  vs nil</p>
<p>    Finance charges	 1.25	  vs 0.52</p>
<p>    Turnover		  60.61	 vs 49.73</p>
<p>    Outside shareholders  0.79	  vs 0.38</p>
<p>Full name: Hicor Ltd</p>
<p>    Net income		1.14	  vs 2.05</p>
<p>NOTE - Clothing to electronics group Hicor Ltd said the decrease in earnings per share for the period was largely due to the higher amount of tax paid after the assessed loss in the clothing division has been fully utliised.</p>
<p>The group added it expected an improvement in the results for the full year to December 1996.</p>
<p>The first half had benefitted from escaping the effects of labour unrest in the clothing sector and experiencing satisfactory demand in textiles, Hicor said</p>
<p>In electronics the group said it would now have 22 stores open by the end of October.</p>
<p>-- Johannesburg newsroom +27 11 482 1003</p>
